---
description: Recording Studio data and API conventions for gems and host apps
alwaysApply: true
---

Prefer Recording Studio public helpers over private registrar internals.

Recordables are immutable snapshots. Use `revise` to change state and `log_event!` for history. Do not write `Event` rows directly.

Declare every configured recordable with `recording_studio_recordable`, including `root` and `allowed_parent_types`. Validate declarations in tests.

Roots are the content and billing buckets. Users and other actors get access to roots; they do not own the tree. Prefer actors over user-only designs. Use Recording Studio Accessible for access — do not invent custom access systems. Grants attach to recordings in the hierarchy and resolve the same way for every actor type. If Accessible cannot cover a scenario, ask how to proceed before writing an alternative.

Put owned tree content in recordings/recordables, recording history in events, and caused-but-unowned operational trails (such as webhook logs) in separate log tables so they do not clog recordings.

Keep addon gems focused and reusable across apps. Prefer extracting shared behavior into `recording-studio-*` gems over one-off app code. Capability mixins are opt-in and do not come for free from type registration.

Get Recording Studio working with defaults first. Prefer gem-owned views and controllers for easy upgrades; let host apps choose routes and override views/controllers only when needed. Keep domain logic in core methods that gem UI, API, and customizations can share. Keep screens simple and one primary action per page, using Flatpack and the core default layout.

For admin UI, use Recording Studio Admin plus Recording Studio Accessible: authenticate in the host app, authorize against an access recording, enable sections on the recordable, and define screens in `app/admin`.

For JSON APIs, use Recording Studio API: register capability actions once, enable them on recordable endpoints, and authorize with Accessible. Do not duplicate UI action logic in custom API controllers.

Preserve public APIs unless the user asks for a breaking change. Use Conventional Commits when a version bump is required.
